! PWM chopper: the first-class TIME-DRIVEN switch circuit element,
! "Component Type = String Switch", in a transient circuit-coupled
! magnetodynamics run.
!
! WHY THIS TEST EXISTS
! --------------------
! The sibling diode tests exercise a SOLUTION-dependent lumped element, which
! needs the coupled Picard loop. A switch is the opposite case: its resistance
! is a prescribed square wave of TIME and does not depend on the solution at
! all, so it must work with NO coupled iterations whatsoever. This test is run
! with "Steady State Max Iterations = 1" on purpose -- that setting is part of
! what is being asserted. If the switch resistance were ever evaluated from the
! solution (or one timestep late), the chopped waveform below would not match
! the independent reference.
!
! CIRCUIT
!   ideal DC source (10 V) -- stranded FEM coil (component 1) -- switch (2)
! all in series. The coil is given an explicit "Resistance = Real 1.0", which
! makes Add_stranded skip the sigma-based FEM resistance, so the loop resistance
! is exactly 1 ohm + R_switch(t). There is NO freewheel path, so when the switch
! opens the coil current is forced through Roff -- see the sibling
! circuits2D_transient_switch_diode_freewheel for the buck cell that adds one.
!
! SWITCH MODEL (time driven, no solution dependence)
!   s(t) = t*f - phase/360 ;  closed iff MODULO(s,1) < duty
!   R = Ron when closed, Roff when open
! Ron = 0.1, Roff = 1e5 (the same decade window the diode tests use; kept
! <= ~1e6 to protect the conditioning of the linear system).
!
! EDGE PLACEMENT. The switching edges are quantised to the timestep grid: R is
! constant across a whole step and is evaluated at the step's END time, which is
! the BDF1 implicit point. With f = 0.2 Hz and dt = T/100 the grid points sit at
! s = n/100. A phase of 1.8 deg = 0.005 of a period moves BOTH edges (the
! closing edge at s = 0.005 and the opening edge at s = 0.405) exactly HALFWAY
! between two grid points, so no grid point ever sits on an edge and the
! closed/open decision can never be flipped by round-off in the accumulated
! time. The resulting schedule is: steps n mod 100 = 1..40 closed, 41..100 open.
! Without the phase offset the closing edge would land exactly on s = 0 and the
! comparison MODULO(s,1) < duty would be a coin flip there.
!
! LINEAR SYSTEM: a DIRECT solve is used, as in the diode tests. The asserted
! instant is an OPEN-phase step where the current is ~2.7e-3 A and the field
! norm is ~5e-6; with an iterative solver that sits at the level of the solve's
! own noise. The same UMFPACK direct solve is used by circuits2D_transient_foil
! under the identical HAVE_UMFPACK/HAVE_MUMPS guard.
!
! CALIBRATION. L is not a free parameter. The identical case with component 2
! replaced by a fixed 1 ohm resistor and the sinusoidal drive of the diode tests
! was run for 250 steps; solving the BDF1 recurrence
! L*(I_n - I_{n-1})/dt = V(t_n) - R_tot*I_n for L step by step gives
! min 2.395782954 H, max 2.395782960 H, median 2.3957829546 H, and replaying the
! recurrence with that constant L reproduces the Elmer currents to 8.1e-13 A on
! an amplitude of 3.17 A. That is an independent re-derivation of the same coil
! inductance the diode tests calibrated (2.3957829553 H, agreeing to 6.8e-10 H).
!
! REFERENCE VALUE
! From the companion switch_pwm_reference.py in this directory, which shares no
! code with Elmer: it replays the scalar BDF1 recurrence
!   I_n = (V0 + a*I_{n-1}) / (a + Rcoil + R_switch(t_n)),   a = L/dt
! on the same time grid. The run stops at step 241 (t = 12.05 s), which is the
! FIRST step of the open phase of the third switching period:
!   I = 2.6975967638e-03 A
! That single number pins BOTH switch states at once, which is why this instant
! was chosen rather than a mid-conduction one:
!   * the numerator is V0 + a*I_240 = 10 + 259.88, i.e. 96.3% of it is the
!     current the CLOSED phase built up (I_240 = 5.4239393974 A), so a wrong
!     on-state or a wrong on-ramp moves the answer almost proportionally;
!   * the denominator is a + Rcoil + Roff = 1.00049e5, i.e. it is Roff that
!     collapses the current by a factor 2010 in a single step -- the open state
!     is asserted directly and quantitatively, not merely as "small".
! For orientation, the reference trajectory around the asserted step is
!   n = 239  t = 11.95  closed  I = 5.3397567578 A
!   n = 240  t = 12.00  closed  I = 5.4239393974 A   (last closed step)
!   n = 241  t = 12.05  open    I = 2.6975967638e-03 A   <== asserted
!   n = 242  t = 12.10  open    I = 1.0124304757e-04 A   (pure Ohm's-law
!                                 leakage V0/(Rcoil+Roff) = 9.99990e-05 A)
! The trajectory is periodic to 10 digits by the third period (step 141 gives
! the same 2.6975967638e-03), so the asserted value is a steady-state property
! of the chopper and not a transient accident.
!
! Based on circuits2D_transient_diode_rectifier.
Check Keywords "Warn"
INCLUDE sif/switch_pwm_circuit.definitions
INCLUDE 1381/mesh.names
Header 1
   Mesh DB "." "1381"
End

$ V0 = 10        ! DC source [V]
$ fsw = 0.2      ! switching frequency [Hz]
$ Tsw = 1/fsw
$ nper = 100     ! timesteps per switching period
$ dt = Tsw/nper
$ nsteps = 241   ! first step of the open phase of the third period
$ duty = 0.4     ! duty cycle
$ phase = 1.8    ! [deg] = 0.005 period, puts both edges midway between steps
$ Ron = 0.1      ! switch on-state resistance [ohm]
$ Roff = 1.0e5   ! switch off-state resistance [ohm]
$ sstol = 1.0e-10

Simulation 1
   Max Output Level = 5
   Coordinate System = Axi symmetric
   Simulation Type = Transient
   Timestepping Method = BDF
   BDF Order = 1
   Timestep Sizes(1) = $ dt
   Timestep Intervals(1) = $ nsteps
!  ONE coupled iteration per timestep. The switch resistance is a function of
!  time only, so there is no fixed point to iterate -- this is the point of the
!  test. Contrast the diode tests, which need 100 here plus relaxation.
   Steady State Max Iterations = 1
End
Solver 1
   Exec Solver = Always
   Equation = Circuits
   Procedure = "CircuitsAndDynamics" "CircuitsAndDynamics"
!  NOTE: neither "Export Circuit Variables" nor
!  "Circuit Variable Relaxation Factor" is set. The switch needs no exported
!  circuit variable and no relaxation; that absence is part of the test.
End
Solver 2
   Equation = "Mag"
   Variable = A
   Procedure = "MagnetoDynamics2D" "MagnetoDynamics2D"
   NonLinear System Relaxation Factor=1
   Export Lagrange Multiplier = Logical True
   Linear System Solver = Direct
   Linear System Direct Method = UMFPACK
   Steady State Convergence Tolerance = $ sstol
   Steady State Convergence Absolute = Logical True
End
Solver 3 ! Result output
   Exec Solver = Never
   Equation = "ResultOutput"
   Procedure = "ResultOutputSolve" "ResultOutputSolver"
   Output File Name = 1381-results
   Vtu format = Logical True
   Save Geometry Ids = Logical True
End
Solver 4
   Exec Solver = Always
   Equation = Circuits Output
   Procedure = "CircuitsAndDynamics" "CircuitsOutput"
End
Solver 5
   Exec Solver = After Timestep
   Equation = "sv"
   Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
   Filename = 1381/dat/switch_pwm.dat
!  The asserted quantity is the SOLVED circuit dof published by CircuitsOutput.
   Show Norm Name = String "res: i_component(2)"
End
Equation 1
   Active Solvers(2) = 2 3
End
Equation 2
   Active Solvers(3) = 1 2 3
End
Material 1
   Name = iron
   Electric Conductivity = 0
   Relative Permeability = 2000
End
Material 2
   Name = air
   Electric Conductivity = 0
   Relative Permeability = 1
End
Material 3
   Name = aluminium
   Relative Permeability = 1
   Electric Conductivity = 1e7
End
Body 1
   Name = Core
   Target Bodies(6) = 1 2 3 4 5 6
   Equation = 1
   Material = 1
End
Body 2
   Name = air
   Target Bodies(2) = 9 7
   Equation = 1
   Material = 2
End
Body 3
   Name = test 1
   Target Bodies = 8
   Equation = 2
   Material = 3
End
Component 1
   Name = String test inductor
   Master Bodies = Integer 3
   Coil Type = String stranded
   Number of Turns = Real 1000
!  Explicit coil resistance: suppresses the FEM sigma-based resistance so that
!  the loop resistance is exactly 1 ohm + R_switch.
   Resistance = Real 1.0
End
Component 2
   Name = String pwm switch
!  The first-class switch. No Master Bodies (it is a pure lumped element) and no
!  dependence on any solution variable.
   Component Type = String Switch
   Switch Frequency = Real $ fsw
   Switch Duty Cycle = Real $ duty
   Switch Phase = Real $ phase
   Switch On Resistance = Real $ Ron
   Switch Off Resistance = Real $ Roff
End
Body Force 1
   Name = "Circuit"
!  Constant DC drive: all the structure in the waveform comes from the switch.
   testsource = Real $ V0
End
Boundary Condition 1
   Target Boundaries(4) = $ center0 center1 center2 center3
   A = Real 0
End
Boundary Condition 2
   Target Boundaries(2) = 4 6
   Name = "Air Gap"
   Air Gap = logical True
   Air Gap Length = Real 0.0001
   Air Gap Relative Permeability = Real 1
End
Boundary Condition 3
   Target Boundaries(7) = 10 13 14 17 18 19 27
   Infinity BC = Logical True
End

! NOTE on the Solver 2 norm. It is NOT the plain A-field norm that the diode
! tests assert. Those run "Steady State Max Iterations = 100", so a steady-state
! ComputeChange runs for the field solver and leaves the FIELD-ONLY norm in the
! variable. Here there is only one coupled iteration, no steady-state
! ComputeChange runs for "mag" at all (only "sv" gets one, see the log), and the
! norm carried into the comparison is the nonlinear-system norm taken over the
! Lagrange-AUGMENTED solution vector, i.e. field dofs plus the six circuit dofs.
! At the asserted open-phase step those circuit dofs dominate it completely:
! v_switch = Roff*I = 269.76 V and v_coil = V0 - v_switch = -259.76 V, and
! sqrt((269.7596763788^2 + 259.7596763788^2 + 10^2)/412) = 18.4565464793 over
! the N = 412 dofs of the augmented system reproduces the value below to 9
! digits -- the field contribution is below the last printed digit. The quantity
! is therefore just as deterministic as the asserted current (it is the same
! solve), and it is the reason the norm jumps by 22.5x at the switch edge
! (0.82154324 at step 240, RELC 1.83 at step 241 -- both visible in the log).
Solver 2 :: Reference Norm = 1.84565465E+01
Solver 2 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 1E-04
! Independent lumped BDF1 reference at t = 12.05 s, see REFERENCE VALUE above.
! Observed deviation: 4.5e-12.
Solver 5 :: Reference Norm = 2.6975967638E-03
Solver 5 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 1E-04
